/bin/

# A `go build` with no -o writes the binary here, named after the module. Anchored with the leading
# slash and no trailing one, which is exactly the right shape and easy to get wrong: a bare `cm` would
# also match the `cmd/cm` source directory and hide the whole command from git, while `/cm/` would
# match only a directory and let the binary through.
#
# This is not hypothetical. A 26 MB build of cm was committed at the repo root and sat in history for
# 57 commits, and every later `go build ./...` silently modified it, so an unrelated change showed a
# dirty binary in `git status`. Removed from history rather than deleted in a new commit, since the blob
# is the cost and a delete keeps it.
/cm

# Without the trailing slash, so a symlink is ignored too.
#
# A git worktree has no third_party of its own, since the libghostty archive is built rather than
# committed, and linking to the main checkout is the cheap way to make a cgo build work there. With
# `/third_party/` that symlink is not ignored and gets committed by an `add -A`.
/third_party
mise.local.toml

# Machine-local agent instructions: a pointer to a skill that lives in someone's dotfiles, and
# personal conventions. Untracked for the same reason it exists, which is that a contributor cloning
# this repo has none of that setup and a checked-in doc naming it would be a dead end. AGENTS.md and
# CONTRIBUTING.md stay self-contained without it.
CLAUDE.local.md
AGENTS.local.md

# Zig's build cache. mise's libghostty task builds inside third_party, which is already ignored, but
# setup-zig in CI points ZIG_LOCAL_CACHE_DIR at the repo root. An untracked directory there makes the
# Go build stamp vcs.modified=true, so every release binary reported its version with a "-dirty"
# suffix while the checkout was in fact clean. Caught by reading the version off a published artifact.
.zig-cache/
